01:16:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a great question, and the experience you’re describing—ears feeling clogged during long ascents or descents, but not usually in a high-speed elevator—is largely about the **rate and duration of pressure change**, and how our bodies keep up with it. ### Why ears get clogged in the first place Your eardrum separates the outer ear from the middle ear. The middle ear is an air-filled space that needs to be at the same air pressure as the outside world for your eardrum to vibrate freely. This equalisation happens through the **Eustachian tube**, a small passage that connects the middle ear to the back of your throat. It’s normally closed, but opens briefly when you swallow, yawn, or chew. When the outside air pressure changes (as it does with altitude), the pressure in your middle ear may not adjust immediately. The eardrum gets pushed inward or outward, becoming stiff and less able to vibrate—that’s the “clogged” or muffled sensation. The
